## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical PKCE understanding in a beginner-friendly way, focusing on correct generation/storage of code_verifier, secure token exchange, and basic CSRF/token-leak mitigations in mobile apps.

## Key Concepts
- OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow with PKCE
- code_verifier/code_challenge generation and secure storage
- Redirect URIs and state for CSRF protection
- Token exchange and TLS requirements
- Token lifecycle: short-lived access tokens, refresh tokens, rotation

## Code Example
```javascript
function generatePKCE() {
  // pseudo-code for clarity; use a crypto-safe RNG in production
  const verifier = base64url(randomBytes(64));
  const challenge = base64url(sha256(verifier));
  return { verifier, challenge };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt PKCE flow for web vs native apps?
- How would you test the PKCE flow end-to-end and simulate interception scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
This question checks practical, beginner-friendly steps to enforce strict per-tenant isolation in a real API stack, not just high-level definitions.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation
- JWT tenant_id verification
- PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS)
- Deny-by-default authorization
- Per-endpoint guardrails and logging
- CI/CD testing for multi-tenant access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js middleware example
function tenantMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const tokenTid = req.user?.tenant_id;
  const pathTid = req.params.tenant_id || req.body.tenant_id;
  if (!pathTid || tokenTid !== pathTid) return res.status(403).send("Forbidden");
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test RLS policies in a CI pipeline?
- How would you monitor for tenant_id leakage in production?
